---
import AppPage from '../../../../components/AppPage.astro';
import { getApp } from '../../../../data/apps';
const app = getApp('kfz-kennzeichen-finder', 'en');
---
<AppPage app={app} lang="en" category="TravelApplication" description="License Plate Finder: where is that car from? More than 1,300 number plate codes from Germany, Austria, Switzerland, Poland, Czechia and Croatia – with map, Wikipedia summary and offline search. Free for Android.">
  <p>Where does the number plate KF come from? What does NWM stand for? And which canton is behind GE? With License Plate Finder you instantly see which city, district or region a car is registered in – for Germany, Austria, Switzerland, Poland, Czechia and Croatia in one app.</p>
  <p>Just type the code and the place appears together with its state, district, canton or region. Perfect for road trips, holidays or a plate-spotting game with the kids.</p>

  <h2>More than 1,300 registration codes from six countries</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>771 codes from Germany</strong>, including reintroduced old codes such as AIB (Bad Aibling) or WAT (Wattenscheid)</li>
    <li><strong>106 district codes from Austria</strong>, e.g. W (Vienna), G (Graz), KB (Kitzbühel)</li>
    <li><strong>26 canton codes from Switzerland</strong>, e.g. ZH (Zurich), BE (Bern), GE (Geneva)</li>
    <li><strong>New: 427 codes from Poland</strong>, e.g. WA (Warsaw), KR (Kraków), DW (Wrocław)</li>
    <li><strong>New: 14 region letters from Czechia</strong>, e.g. A (Prague), B (Brno), P (Plzeň)</li>
    <li><strong>New: all town codes from Croatia</strong>, e.g. ZG (Zagreb), ST (Split), RI (Rijeka)</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A detail page for every code</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Map showing where the place is</li>
    <li>Short summary from Wikipedia in your language, with a link to the full article</li>
    <li>New: places and regions in your language – Munich instead of München, Prague instead of Praha</li>
    <li>Hear the place name read aloud – in the language of the place, so Genève in French and Praha in Czech</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Quick and simple</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Search works without internet</li>
    <li>Alphabetical list of all codes with place search – also finds the original name</li>
    <li>Switch country with one tap</li>
    <li>Light and dark mode</li>
    <li>In English, German, French, Italian, Polish, Czech and Croatian</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Examples</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>HH → Hamburg</li>
    <li>M → Munich</li>
    <li>KF → Kaufbeuren (Bavaria)</li>
    <li>W → Vienna</li>
    <li>GE → Geneva</li>
    <li>WA → Warsaw</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  <h3>Does the app work offline?</h3>
  <p>Yes. The search and the list of all codes work without internet. Only the map and the Wikipedia summary on the detail page need a connection.</p>
  <h3>Are old codes included?</h3>
  <p>Yes, the reintroduced old German codes are included, for example AIB (Bad Aibling), WOR (Wolfratshausen) or SOG (Schongau).</p>
  <h3>How much does the app cost?</h3>
  <p>The app is free and funded by ads. Your home country is always free, plus one more country every day. Unlock the other countries for 24 hours by watching a short video, or permanently with the one-time purchase “Remove ads” (€2.49 in Germany) – no subscription. The purchase unlocks all countries and also removes the ads.</p>
  <h3>Where does the data come from?</h3>
  <p>From the Wikipedia lists of vehicle registration codes of the six countries. The data is checked and updated regularly, most recently in November 2026.</p>
</AppPage>
